  "sources": [
    {
      "title": "MDR on UPI unlikely to trigger shift back to cash usage: RBI's Murmu",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/banking/mdr-on-upi-unlikely-to-trigger-shift-back-to-cash-usage-rbi-s-murmu-126091801158_1.html",
      "description": "RBI Deputy Governor S C Murmu says MDR will help the payments ecosystem recover costs, while cash continues to retain a significant role as a store of value",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-18T13:52:17"
    },
    {
      "title": "UPI MDR apprehension unlikely to spur higher cash usage: RBI DG Murmu",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/finance/news/upi-mdr-apprehension-unlikely-to-spur-higher-cash-usage-rbi-dg-murmu-126091800863_1.html",
      "description": "Concerns that introducing a Merchant Discount Rate (MDR) would spur a surge in cash transactions are unlikely to materialise, RBI Deputy Governor Shirish Chandra Murmu said on Friday, adding that it was only an \"initial apprehension\".\nFrom October 15, a 0.4 per cent MDR will apply to person-to-merchant UPI payments above Rs 2,000. The charge will be paid by merchants, not consumers, and will be capped at Rs 300 for transactions of Rs 75,000 or more.\nPayments between individuals, as well as the vast majority of everyday merchant payments, will remain free.\n\"I don't think that apprehension will come true. It will be just initial apprehension,\" Murmu said while addressing a financial market conclave hosted here by BCC&amp;I on whether more cash would be used because of MDR.\n\"Because it is a major shift in terms of how basically you are recovering the cost. So, that's why it may be apprehension only. I don't think this will have any impact, unlike some voices about whether cash will go up",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-18T12:40:02"
    },
    {
      "title": "UPI MDR wins payments industry support, but merchants fear higher costs",
      "link": "https://www.business-standard.com/industry/banking/upi-mdr-returns-rs-2000-merchants-retailers-petrol-pumps-banks-126091800962_1.html",
      "description": "The 0.4 per cent charge on merchant UPI payments above Rs 2,000 could generate up to Rs 20,600 crore annually, but retailers and fuel dealers fear margin pressure and a possible shift towards cash",
      "source_name": "Business Standard Latest",
      "published": "2026-09-18T12:15:04"
    }
  ]
